format 1
adapter curriculum 1
unit: radiation-meets-matter

objective u3-gamma-shielding

statement:
    Know that gamma rays pass through thin barriers and need thick, dense material such as lead or concrete, and that the right shield depends on the kind of radiation.

misconception: Anything solid is a good shield against all radiation.

item u3-l2-choice-apron
type: choice
surfaces: lesson
surfaces: practice
surfaces: review
references: ref-epa-protecting-yourself

prompt:
    Why does a dentist put a lead apron on you for an X-ray?

options:
    {"id":"stop","label":"Lead is a barrier that protects against X-rays"}
    {"id":"warm","label":"It keeps you warm during the visit","note":"The apron is there as a barrier against X-rays."}
    {"id":"clean","label":"It keeps dust off your clothes","note":"The apron is there as a barrier against X-rays, not against dust."}

answer: stop

explanation:
    Lead is a dense material, so it works as a barrier against X-rays, the same way it does against gamma rays.

item u3-l2-choice-gamma
type: choice
surfaces: lesson
surfaces: practice
surfaces: review
references: ref-epa-radiation-basics
references: ref-openstax-chem-21-6

prompt:
    What makes a good shield against gamma rays?

options:
    {"id":"thick","label":"A thick layer of a dense material, such as lead or concrete"}
    {"id":"paper","label":"A sheet of paper","note":"Gamma rays pass straight through paper."}
    {"id":"cloth","label":"Ordinary clothing","note":"Gamma rays pass through clothing. They need something thick and dense."}
    {"id":"thin","label":"A thin sheet of metal","note":"A thin layer of metal stops beta particles, but gamma rays go through it."}

answer: thick

explanation:
    Gamma rays are very penetrating, so the shield has to be both thick and dense. Several inches of lead or a few feet of concrete will do it.

item u3-l2-fill-gamma
type: fill
surfaces: lesson
surfaces: practice
surfaces: review
references: ref-epa-radiation-basics

prompt:
    To stop gamma rays you need a thick layer of a ___ material, such as lead.

mode: bank

bank:
    {"id":"dense","label":"dense"}
    {"id":"light","label":"light"}
    {"id":"clear","label":"clear"}

answer: dense

explanation:
    Dense material has a lot of matter packed into it, and that is what a gamma ray has to get through. Lead and concrete are dense.

item u3-l2-tom-gamma-paper
type: true_or_myth
surfaces: lesson
surfaces: practice
surfaces: review
references: ref-epa-radiation-basics

statement:
    A sheet of paper stops gamma rays.

answer: myth

explanation:
    Myth. Gamma rays pass through paper, and through skin and clothing too. It takes several inches of lead or a few feet of concrete to stop them.

item u3-l2-tom-thin-metal
type: true_or_myth
surfaces: lesson
surfaces: practice
surfaces: review
references: ref-epa-radiation-basics

statement:
    A thin layer of metal is enough to stop gamma rays.

answer: myth

explanation:
    Myth. A thin layer of metal stops beta particles, but gamma rays pass through it. They need several inches of lead or a few feet of concrete.

item u3-x-gamma-barrier
type: choice
surfaces: practice
surfaces: checkpoint
surfaces: review
references: ref-epa-protecting-yourself

prompt:
    Which of these is used as a barrier against gamma rays?

options:
    {"id":"paper","label":"A stack of paper","note":"Gamma rays go through paper. It is not dense or thick enough."}
    {"id":"concrete","label":"A thick concrete wall"}
    {"id":"foil","label":"A sheet of aluminum foil","note":"A thin layer of metal stops beta particles, but not gamma rays."}

answer: concrete

explanation:
    Concrete is dense, and a thick wall of it protects against gamma rays. Lead and water are used in the same way.

item u3-x-tom-shield-materials
type: true_or_myth
surfaces: practice
surfaces: checkpoint
surfaces: review
references: ref-epa-protecting-yourself

statement:
    Some radioactive materials are stored under water or in lead-lined rooms.

answer: true

explanation:
    True. Water, concrete and lead are barriers that protect against gamma rays and X-rays, so they are used to store and shield radioactive materials.
